Actually, it seems that other hash implementations rely on the use of the chain style when an insertion occurs in an already used bucket, and the performance of insertion and retrieval is satisfactory.

So... what if I ask a different question on the matter? What if I used a sparse approach? Allocate only what I need when I need it.

Mind you that there will be an allocated space of memory to speed up its allocations. But buckets are only created when they are needed. They are allocated to a dynamically sizing pool array and will get reused.

    class SpatialHashBucket {
        private:
        int ID;
        int Grid_Stored_Ids;
        int *Grid_ID_Index; //Populated after the insertion phase, stores an index to other grids.
        int *buffer;
        public:
        ...
    }

And each bucket simply stores a few extra bits of data. The implementation is BigO(N) all around, but I have found it to be incredibly fast, still faster than bouncing around on too many pointers. With all allocations happening on a buffer of pre-slotted memory, the framerates won't dip too much.

I can always try to use another map library... but I don't know any beyond the standard library that allows for multiple items to have the same collision. Nor do I know any that allows a custom hash function.

How does that simple shader code work exactly? O.o;

It seems pretty straightforward to me the way it is.

But thanks, I didn't consider using a really large buffer. I assume you mark it with the STREAM flag?
